            <physDesc xml:id="idm34">
               <titlePage label="Title (folder, fol. 1r)" xml:id="titlePage_0ae9ad72">
                  <p xml:id="idm36">18 [changed in ink to:] 19<rend xml:id="idm37" rend="sup"> 2.a.</rend> | II. Sonaten | a | 2 Claviere und Pedal | von | J. A. Scheibe | Capelmeister in Copenhagen | 1742.</p>
               </titlePage>
               <titlePage label="First music page, Sonata 1 (fol. 1v)" xml:id="titlePage_ef0a7dfb">
                  <p xml:id="idm39">[top right margin:] Copenhagen d 16 Marz | 1742. | Sonate mit 2 Clavieren und Pedal verf: v. J. A. Scheibe.</p>
               </titlePage>
               <titlePage label="Ninth music page, Sonata 2 (fol. 5v)" xml:id="titlePage_e0ecfc63">
                  <p xml:id="idm41">[top right margin:] Copenhagen d 17 Marz | 1742. | Sonate mit 2 Clavieren und Pedal, verf: v. J. A. Scheibe.</p>
               </titlePage>
               <extent xml:id="idm42">5 bifolios</extent>
               <dimensions xml:id="idm43" unit="cm">33.9x21.3</dimensions>
               <watermark xml:id="watermark_4143724a">folder: double-headed eagle with sceptre and sword and unidentifiable countermark (cf. Gravell:4290); paper: 'C6', the royal monogram of King Christian VI (reigned 1730–46) and countermark consisting of  'ID' (Johann Drewsen; cf. Voorn:IV, 6); paper of good quality.</watermark>
               <physMedium xml:id="idm45">2<rend xml:id="idm46" rend="sup">o</rend>; upright format, consisting of 3 and 2 nested bifolios; blank page (fol. 1r), sonata 1 (fols. 1v–5r), sonata 2 (fols. 5v–10r), blank page (fol. 10v); in all 5 bifolios. Hand-ruled paper, 18 staves (8mm; single rastrum used) in light brown ink and music in black ink. A fairly clean copy with a few corrections and changes in same black ink as well as some in a lighter ink; an addition in foreign hand of five bars appears below the finale of the third movement of sonata 1. Throughout the source crosses have been added regularly at bar lines which most likely stem from either a transcription, possibly that carried out by Wegener around 1850, or a planned publication by Breitkopf.</physMedium>
            </physDesc>
